# --- okf-server: WHOLE-CORPUS TOPIC-LINK AUTHORING (Story 1-8g-p) -----------
# The import wizard authors the cross-links a corpus that never cites itself is
# born without (a corpus of bulletins imports with an empty graph otherwise,
# and graph retrieval then returns isolated hits). The SAME pass is available
# on demand from the editor (Step 7 "Cross-link topics"), so a repository built
# before this feature can be cross-linked without re-importing. It is GENERIC:
# candidate pairs come from token overlap + an assistant pass over title and
# summary, with no lexicon and no domain assumption — bean bulletins, asteroids
# and statute sections all cross-link through the same code path. Re-running is
# safe: existing links are kept and only new ones are added.
# OKF_TOPIC_LINK_ENABLED=true
#   Default: true (the import runs the pass automatically). Set 0 to skip it
#   import-time — the assistant calls are the only cost worth avoiding.
#   Compose: yes. Ansible: yes (guarded).
# OKF_TOPIC_LINK_TIMEOUT_MS=60000
#   Default: 60000 per assistant call (a large batch prompt takes ~25s).
#   Compose: yes (empty passthrough). Ansible: guarded.
# OKF_TOPIC_LINK_LANES=4
#   Default: 4 concurrent assistant calls. Lower it when the GPU is shared and
#   you want the pass to queue rather than compete for the model server.
#   Compose: yes (empty passthrough). Ansible: guarded.
# OKF_TOPIC_LINK_BATCH=8
#   Default: 8 concepts per assistant call (prompt size vs call count).
#   Compose: yes (empty passthrough). Ansible: guarded.
# OKF_TOPIC_LINK_MAX_PER_CONCEPT=6
#   Default: 6 links written per page (graph density; higher = denser but
#   noisier traversal). Compose: yes (empty passthrough). Ansible: guarded.

