# --- RAGAS benchmarking in the OKF Lab (Story 8-6, ADR-okf-043) ---
# The ragas-service Python sidecar owns testset generation, metric
# evaluation, and the xlsx interchange with tests/rag-benchmarks; the
# okf-server orchestrates. NO feature is indexed on a specific model —
# the judge/embedder vars point at the deployment's chosen OpenAI-
# compatible endpoints and default to the deployment's LLM config; the
# per-task capability minimums live in the spec's model-requirements
# companion (guided JSON, window sizes, no reasoning-token leak).
# OKF_RAGAS_URL=http://ragas-service:8090
#   Default: http://ragas-service:8090. The sidecar's base URL as okf-server
#   resolves it on the overlay. Unreachable = honest 503 RAGAS_UNAVAILABLE
#   per Lab route. Used by: okf-server (ragas-suite-service).
# RAGAS_INTERNAL_SECRET=<shared secret with the sidecar>
#   Default: OKF_INTERNAL_SECRET (the dataprep-callback contract — one
#   deployment-wide internal secret). Fail-closed: unset rejects everything.
#   Used by: okf-server -> ragas-service auth. Compose: yes. Ansible: yes.
# RAGAS_JUDGE_BASE_URL=http://vllm:8000/v1
#   Default: the deployment's vLLM endpoint. OpenAI-compatible judge/generator
#   endpoint — any local server qualifies (vLLM, LiteLLM, ollama).
# RAGAS_JUDGE_MODEL=<model id>
#   Default: VLLM_LLM_MODEL_ID. The judge+generator model. Must meet the
#   model-requirements minimums (strict JSON output; window sized for
#   question+answer+contexts; no reasoning tokens in completions).
# RAGAS_JUDGE_API_KEY=<bearer for the judge endpoint>
#   Default: VLLM_API_KEY.
# RAGAS_JUDGE_CONTEXT_TOKENS=<the judge model's context window>
#   Default: VLLM_MAX_MODEL_LEN (65536 if unset). The benchmark KG-input cap
#   derives from it (RAGAS_DOC_CHAR_CAP=auto): (window - 3072) / 2 pair-safe
#   tokens * 4 chars/token, clamped by RAGAS_DOC_CHAR_MAX — so transform
#   prompts fit whatever model is selected, local or frontier.
# RAGAS_DOC_CHAR_CAP=<chars | auto | 0>
#   Default: auto (derive from the window above). Explicit number wins; 0
#   disables (the relationship builder joins concept PAIRS — only disable
#   if you know every concept is small).
# RAGAS_DOC_CHAR_MAX=<chars>
#   Default: 48000. Auto-mode ceiling — huge windows stop here so generation
#   runtime stays sane.
# RAGAS_EMBED_BASE_URL=http://tei-embedding-server:80
#   Default: the TEI embedding service host. Required for answer_relevancy
#   and testset generation (KG transforms embed); unset drops those
#   honestly with a warning.
# RAGAS_EMBED_API=tei
#   Default: tei — POSTs {RAGAS_EMBED_BASE_URL}/embed with {inputs,
#   truncate} (the project's TEI-behind-nginx contract; the OpenAI-style
#   /v1/embeddings route is NOT exposed by those nginxes). Set openai for
#   any OpenAI-compatible embeddings endpoint (LiteLLM, external APIs).
# RAGAS_EMBED_MODEL=<embedding model id>
#   Default: EMBEDDING_MODEL_ID. Consistent within a run; matching the corpus
#   embedder is recommended but not required for answer_relevancy.
# OKF_RAGAS_TESTSET_SIZE_DEFAULT=20
#   Default: 20. The generate form's initial size; the user sets the actual
#   dimensions per generation (size, distribution weights, personas).
# OKF_RAGAS_MAX_ENTRIES=200
#   Default: 200. Hard cap per suite (generation + import).
# OKF_RAGAS_RUN_CONCURRENCY=2
#   Default: 2. Per-run query concurrency — bounds LLM load on the shared
#   GPU so benchmarking does not starve chat answering.
# OKF_RAGAS_RERANK_CONCURRENCY=3
#   Default: 3. How many benchmark rerank batch calls (32 texts each) may be
#   in flight at once. Batch calls are independent and scores are identical
#   at any concurrency — this is transport, not computation. 1 = the old
#   sequential behaviour; 3 overlaps a 192-chunk pool (6 batches) into ~2
#   waves, ~3x faster retrieval leg. Global in-flight ceiling is
#   OKF_RAGAS_RUN_CONCURRENCY x this value — raise on an idle GPU, lower to
#   1 when the benchmark shares the GPU with live chat traffic.
# OKF_RAGAS_SERVE_URL=http://chatqna-xeon-backend-server:8888/v1/chatqna
#   Default: the compose chatqna service. SERVE mode (the benchmark default)
#   posts each question to this URL - the same pipeline a citizen question
#   takes - and measures the served answer and served contexts. Only a
#   'whatif' run (knob experiments) ignores it.
# OKF_RAGAS_ANSWER_MAX_TOKENS=5000
#   Default: 5000. The answer leg's INITIAL token budget. It was hardcoded
#   to 800, and a reasoning-capable model burns 400-700 tokens BEFORE
#   writing a word -- so every answer hit the length cap, was thrown away,
#   and was re-called at 3200 by the adaptive ladder. Two calls per question
#   with the first one entirely wasted (live 2026-10-22: the answer leg's
#   rate fell from 10 questions/min to 2 as the harder questions arrived).
#   max_tokens is a CAP, not a reservation -- a model that needs less never
#   pays for it, and the ladder still climbs for the ones that do.
# OKF_RAGAS_LONG_TIMEOUT_MS=3600000
#   Default: 3600000 (1h). LIVENESS HORIZON, not a job deadline: a
#   reachable, running job is NEVER timed out — the horizon bounds only how
#   long an UNREACHABLE sidecar is retried before the job record fails
#   honestly. The only things that end a healthy long job are its own
#   completion or the user's Cancel (never a timer). Used by: okf-server.
# RAGAS_LLM_CACHE=1
#   Default: 1 (on). Disk judge cache — responses keyed by model+prompt+
#   params persist under RAGAS_LLM_CACHE_DIR, so a repeat generation over
#   the same repositories skips the whole knowledge-graph phase (minutes to
#   seconds) and re-runs of a suite re-judge only what changed. 0 disables.
#   Used by: ragas-service.
# RAGAS_LLM_CACHE_DIR=/tmp/ragas-llm-cache
#   Default: /tmp/ragas-llm-cache (inside the container — survives job
#   churn, cleared when the container is recreated). Cache IO is
#   best-effort: a cache failure can never fail a job.
# RAGAS_LLM_CACHE_LEGACY=0
#   Default: 0 (off). Migration switch for the 2026-10-21 cache-key fix:
#   the judge identity in the cache key now carries the ENDPOINT as well as
#   the model, so a repointed judge can never replay another endpoint's
#   answers — at the price of a cold cache, and the entries written by an
#   earlier deployment become unreachable. Set 1 ONLY when the judge
#   endpoint has NOT changed since those entries were written: each read
#   then falls back to the legacy key and MIGRATES the entry to the new key
#   (the cache heals itself, one answer at a time). Leave 0 on a deployment
#   whose judge was repointed — that is the case the fix protects.
#   Used by: ragas-service.
# RAGAS_LLM_MAX_CONCURRENCY=8
#   Default: 8. How many judge calls may be in flight at once. The
#   upstream answered 82 of ~500 synthesis calls with 429 Too Many
#   Requests and the throttled questions were dropped (live 2026-10-21:
#   a 100-question suite arrived with 63). ragas' own executor runs 16
#   workers; this is the real ceiling, and it is what the upstream's rate
#   limit has to accommodate. Lower it on a stricter or shared plan.
#   Used by: ragas-service.
# RAGAS_LLM_MAX_RETRIES=5
#   Default: 5. How many times a rate-limited (429) call is retried
#   before the question is lost. The backoff is exponential WITH jitter
#   and honours Retry-After — the openai SDK's own retry has no jitter,
#   so a throttled batch came back as a synchronised herd and was
#   throttled again. 0 disables the retry.
#   Used by: ragas-service.
# RAGAS_DO_NOT_TRACK=true
#   Default: true. ragas's own usage telemetry — OFF. ragas 0.4.3 POSTs
#   every prompt's usage event to https://t.explodinggradients.com from
#   INSIDE the async prompt path, with a 1 s timeout, so it blocks the
#   event loop: the 16 workers ragas asks for and RAGAS_LLM_MAX_CONCURRENCY
#   never overlap. Measured in the sidecar (2026-10-26): a warm CACHE HIT
#   — no model call at all — cost 0.79 s, and 0.008 s with this off. That
#   tax was ~50 min of Solar System's ~3,800-prompt knowledge-graph build,
#   and the same tax is charged on every judge call of every scored row.
#   It is also a SOVEREIGNTY question: a public-sector deployment must not
#   ship per-call usage telemetry to a third-party endpoint outside the
#   deployment. Set false only deliberately.
#   Used by: ragas-service.
# RAGAS_EVAL_MAX_WORKERS=8
#   Default: 8. How many judge calls ragas' own executor may run at once
#   during the SCORING leg. This was hardcoded to 8 — the same number as
#   RAGAS_LLM_MAX_CONCURRENCY, so the two ceilings could not be moved
#   apart. On a judge plan with no throttling the pool is the real limit:
#   100 rows x ~13 judge calls at 8-way concurrency measured ~5.4 min per
#   row (live 2026-10-22: 17 rows in 92 minutes, ~8 h projected).
#   Raise it together with RAGAS_EVAL_BATCH_ROWS — a single row only has
#   ~13 calls to offer the pool, so the pool cannot be filled by the pool
#   size alone.
#   Used by: ragas-service.
# RAGAS_EVAL_BATCH_ROWS=1
#   Default: 1. How many rows are scored per evaluate() call. 1 makes the
#   progress bar tick per question; 2 still ticks in twos and roughly
#   doubles the work available to the worker pool. Raise it with
#   RAGAS_EVAL_MAX_WORKERS, not on its own.
#   Used by: ragas-service.
# RAGAS_EVAL_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=600
#   Default: 600. The per-call budget for a scoring call. A call that
#   exceeds it raises TimeoutError inside ragas' executor and — because
#   evaluation runs with raise_exceptions=False — that metric then scores
#   NULL for the row, without an error on the run (live 2026-10-22: one
#   such timeout in the first 45 min of a 100-row run). A judge call that
#   takes this long is stuck rather than slow, so keep this well under the
#   client's own 300 s HTTP timeout. 30 is the floor.
#   Used by: ragas-service.

